  <document-link format="APPLICATION/PDF" url="https://iatipublisher-prod.s3.amazonaws.com/document-link/87/Annex%202C%20The%20Journey%20of%20Youth-Led%20Partners%20in%20Kenya1719392167.pdf">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Journey of Youth-Led Partners in Kenya</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">A key element of our approach to partnerships and civil society strengthening is supporting local partners and their capacities as organizations. The growth of the YLPs has come through continuous mentorship, role modelling and CS. Each year min. 5% of the programme is earmarked for the partners&#x2019; organizational development and CS meeting their assessed needs, strategic priorities and interests of their organizations.</narrative>
   </description>
   <category code="A08"></category>
   <language code="EN"></language>
   <document-date iso-date="2024-06-30"></document-date>
  </document-link>
  <document-link format="APPLICATION/PDF" url="https://planbornefondenimpact.dk/wp-content/uploads/2023/06/Case-study-Gender-Transformative-.pdf">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Youth Driving Change (Strengthening our programmes&#x2019; Gender Transformative potential)</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The programme has been designed in line with Plan International&#x2019;s Gender Transformative Approach (GTA) and the organization&#x2019;s Gender Transformative Marker (GTM) has been applied to seven country programmes (CP), assessing their gender transformative potential and identifying areas in need of strengthening. An in-depth analysis of the seven GTMs has confirmed the CP&#x2019;s generally strong gender transformative potential, but has also revealed a certain level of inconsistency in how the GTM is applied that impacts the tool&#x2019;s effectiveness, as well as elements of the GTA that are less well applied than others.</narrative>
   </description>
   <category code="A08"></category>
   <language code="EN"></language>
   <document-date iso-date="2023-06-30"></document-date>
  </document-link>
  <result aggregation-status="1" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output A - # of direct Southern partners</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">This indicator covers direct Southern partners. These are organisations with a contractual relationship with the project or organisations funded through the project. This only pertains to Southern-based organisations funded or supported within the current Strategic Partnership Agreement period between 2022 and 2025. This is a standard output indicator under the Strategic Partnership Agreement with the Danish Ministry of Foreign Affairs. It seeks to provide a sense of scale across change contributions funded by the agreement. Narrative case studies compliment the indicator to capture change contributions.</narrative>
   </description>
   <document-link format="APPLICATION/HTTP" url="https://planbornefondenimpact.dk/strategic-partnership-2022-2025-with-danida/">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strategic Partnership 2022-2025 with DANIDA</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">PlanB&#xf8;rnefonden has signed a new strategic partnership agreement with the Danish Ministry of Foreign Affairs (DANIDA) to continue development work and humanitarian efforts in fragile settings in West and East Africa.

Our Strategic Partnership 2022-2025 consists of eight country programmes implemented in countries which are highly vulnerable to climate change and have restricted civic space, not least in urban areas, where young activists have been subjected to severe harassment by police, while responding to the Covid-19 pandemic. Our programmes take place in fragile rural, urban and displacement settings, where children and young people, particularly girls and young women, are the most at risk.

Four of country programmes take place in fragile contexts (Burkina Faso, Ethiopia, Mali, and Zimbabwe), one country programme targets urban refugees and host communities (Uganda), and the remaining three country programmes are implemented in settings that are increasingly subject to the negative effects of climate change (Benin, Togo, and Kenya).</narrative>
    </description>
    <category code="A08"></category>
    <document-date iso-date="2022-12-31"></document-date>
   </document-link>
